Side-finding from the #9 arch audit.

lib/OCP/Hetzner.pm:36
  my $arch = $filter{architecture} // "x86";
filters WWW::Hetzner::Cloud server types to x86 only, so Hetzners ARM line (CAX*, architecture "arm") would never appear in the picker.

Latent, not an active bug: server_type_options has no callers at all right now (grep over lib/ bin/ finds only its own definition), so nothing is being filtered today. When it does get wired into `ocp init`, the default must not silently hide arm64 machines — either offer the architecture as a choice or default to showing both.

Same file, same shape: location_options is fine.
Done: both architectures are shown by default, and the architecture stays available as a deliberate filter.

The default is gone rather than flipped: architecture is undef unless the caller passes it, so the grep only narrows when asked. Passing architecture => arm or x86 still works for a caller that knows what it wants. Deprecated types are still filtered out.

Two supporting changes so a mixed list is usable. The label now names the architecture, cax11 (arm, 2 CPU, 4GB, 40GB shared), otherwise a picker showing both lines gives no way to tell CAX from CPX. And the sort gained a name tiebreak after cores and memory: with one architecture the pairs never collided, with both they do, and Perl sort is not stable, so equally sized machines could swap places between calls.

location_options untouched, it was already the right shape.

Still latent by design: neither picker has a caller yet. Filed as a follow-up rather than done here, since wiring it means touching ocp init prompting, which is a different change.

Files: lib/OCP/Hetzner.pm. Tests: t/42-hetzner-options.t against inline fake server types, covering both-by-default, arm-only and x86-only filters, the architecture in the label, and deterministic ordering. Changes bullet added.
